mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — repurposing end to end\n\nTwo worked flows: one atom fanned out to five native versions, and a long-form piece atomized.\nVoice is the blunt indie-founder voice from earlier skills (short, dry, no exclamation marks,\nconcrete). Your output uses the loaded `voice.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — one atom → five native versions\n\n**The atom:** we deleted our most-requested feature because nobody used it; the \"boring\"\nreplacement became the most-used in two days. Lesson: build less, watch behavior not requests.\n\n### Instagram — Reel\n- **Hook (visual):** hand hitting \"delete\" on a block of code. **On-screen:** \"Deleting 6 months\n  of work.\" **Spoken:** \"We just deleted our most-requested feature.\"\n- Beats: the why → the boring fix → the result. **CTA:** save this for your next roadmap meeting.\n\n### LinkedIn — text post\n- **Hook line:** \"'Requested' is the most expensive word in product.\"\n- Whitespace-formatted, expands the lesson (built for words, not behavior), ends on a genuine\n  question. **CTA:** \"Anyone actually build straight from requests and have it work?\" Link in\n  comments if any.\n\n### X — single post\n- \"'Customers requested it' is how you justify six months building something nobody uses.\n  Deleted ours today. Watching behavior from now on.\"\n- Two lines, the point up front. No hashtags.\n\n### Instagram — carousel\n- Cover: \"We deleted our most-requested feature →\". Slides: 1) the ask vs the use, 2) requested ≠\n  used, 3) build the boring thing, 4) shipping less is a feature. **CTA:** save.\n\n### Pinterest — pin\n- Keyword-rich: \"Product roadmap lesson: why customer requests mislead | build less\". Image: the\n  \"requested vs used\" contrast graphic. Links to the full blog. Evergreen, searchable.\n\n**Notice:** same atom, five genuinely different builds — different hooks, formats, lengths, CTAs,\nregister. None is a copy-paste of another.\n\n**The bad version (avoid):** the LinkedIn paragraph pasted identically into IG, X, and a Pinterest\ndescription. Fits none, looks like spam to anyone following on two, and underperforms everywhere.\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — long-form → many atoms\n\n**Source:** a 1,800-word blog post, \"10 things we learned in our first year.\"\n\n**Atomize (don't summarize):** pull the strongest standalone atoms —\n1. the deleted-feature lesson (→ the five posts above),\n2. \"our first hire was a mistake — here's the signal we missed,\"\n3. \"the pricing change that doubled revenue,\"\n4. \"the channel we wasted 6 months on,\"\n5. \"the one metric we should've watched sooner.\"\n\nEach atom → its own native post(s) across platforms. One blog → ~3 weeks of content. Optionally,\none post points to the full blog (the hero link), but every atom stands alone.\n\n**Anti-pattern:** \"Just published a new blog: 10 things we learned 👇 [link]\" as the *only* post.\nThat throws away nine atoms.\n\n---\n\n## What both examples show\n\n- **Atomize first**, then build native — don't translate a wrapper.\n- Each version is **different and stands alone**.\n- **Voice constant, register shifts** per platform.\n- Long-form yields **many** posts, not one summary.\n- Quality per platform is the gate; volume is the byproduct.\n"
